        >
          afi
        </a>
        <p
          dangerouslySetInnerHTML={
            {
              "__html": "a, m. [cp. Lat. <i>avus,</i> Ulf. <i>avô</i> = <i>μάμμα</i>, and <i>aba</i> = <i>ανήρ, vir</i>], <i>grandfather:</i> it is now frequent, but occurs very rarely in old writers, who almost always use móðurfaðir or föðurfaðir. Yet it occurs in the poem Rm. 16—afi ok amma—and Vþm. 29, where it = föðurfaðir. It is curious to observe that in the poem Skm.—whence it is again transferred into the Grógaldr—it is used in the sense of <i>a boy</i> or <i>a son;</i> cp. as an illustration of this use the Norse phrase—D. N. iv. 848—afi eptir afa = <i>son after father, man after man</i> in uninterrupted succession, in accord- ance to the Gothic <i>aba</i>; Edda 108, Fms. iv. 288, vi. 346, xi. 6. We also say lang-afi, <i>great-grandfather,</i> and langamma, <i>great-grandmother.</i>",
            }
          }
        />
      </li>
