        <p
          dangerouslySetInnerHTML={
            {
              "__html": "adj., fem. blá, neut. blátt, [Scot. <i>bla,</i> which has the Icel. sense of <i>dark blue, livid:</i> cp. A. S. <i>bleov;</i> Engl. <i>blue;</i> Germ. <i>blau;</i> Swed.-Dan. <i>blå:</i> cp. also A. S. <i>bleo</i> = <i>colour</i>], prop. Lat. <i>lividus;</i> of the colour of lead, Snót 231; blár sem Hel, cp. Engl. <i>black as death,</i> Eb. 314, cp. Edda 13; of the livid colour caused by a blow, in the alliterative phrase, blár ok blóðugr, Korm. 108; sárir eða lostnir svá blátt eðr rautt sé eptir, Grág. ii. 13: blár is the colour of mourning, tjalda blám reflum, Fms. xi. 17; falda blá, <i>to wrap the head in black,</i> Ísl. ii. 351 (in a verse); cp. kolblár, Blámaðr, etc.; blár logi, <i>a pale ‘lowe,’</i> of a witch’s flame, Gullþ. 5: of cloths; möttull, Nj. 24; kápa, 255; kyrtill, 184; mörk, <i>stripes,</i> Ld. 244.",
            }
          }
        />
      </li>
