          fastr
        </a>
        <p
          dangerouslySetInnerHTML={
            {
              "__html": "<strong>1.</strong> adj. [wanting in Ulf., who renders βέβαιος etc. by <i>tulgus;</i> but common to all other Teut. idioms; A. S. <i>fæst;</i> Engl. <i>fast;</i> O. H. G. <i>fasti;</i> Germ. <i>fest</i>; Swed.-Dan. <i>fast</i>]:—<i>fast, firm,</i> esp. with the notion of <i>sticking fast</i> to the spot; hrútr f. (<i>held fast, entangled</i>) á meðal viða, 655 vii. 2; fastr á velli, <i>standing fast,</i> e. g. in a battle, Fms. xi. 246; vera, standa f. fyrir, <i>to stand fast,</i> Þorst. St. 53; f. á fótum, of a bondsman whose <i>feet are bound fast to the soil,</i> Grág. ii. 192, Nj. 27: grið-fastr, <i>home-bound,</i> of a servant: the phrase, e-t er fast fyrir, <i>a thing is hard to win, difficult,</i> Lv. 94, Fms. xi. 32, Ld. 154.",
            }
          }
        />
