          fíll
        </a>
        <p
          dangerouslySetInnerHTML={
            {
              "__html": "m. [early Swed. and Dan. <i>fil</i>], <i>an elephant;</i> this interesting word, which is still in exclusive use in Icel., was borrowed from the Persian <i>fil,</i> and came to Scandinavia in early times, probably by the eastern road of trade through Russia and Constantinople; it occurs in a verse of the 10th century (Fb. i. 209), the genuineness of which may be doubtful, but at all events the word is old; freq. in Al., Stj., Flóv., and romances. But úlfaldi, Goth. <i>ulbandus,</i> A. S. <i>olfend</i> or <i>olvend,</i> a corruption of the Gr. ελέφαντ-, means <i>camel.</i>",
            }
          }
        />
      </li>
