          onClick={[Function]}
          onMouseEnter={[Function]}
          onTouchStart={[Function]}
        >
          gengr
        </a>
        <p
          dangerouslySetInnerHTML={
            {
              "__html": "adj. <i>able to walk,</i> Grág. ii. 33, Fms. vii. 208, Landn. 226:—<i>passable, fit to walk,</i> Bs. i. 322; ó-gengr, <i>unfit to walk</i> or <i>impassable;</i> íll-g., <i>bad to pass,</i> of ice, crags, or the like:—the phrase, eiga heiman-gengt, <i>to be able to go from home,</i> i. e. <i>to have leisure,</i> Fbr. 17 new Ed.; eg á ekki heiman-gengt, ílla heiman-gengt, etc.:—of money, <i>good,</i> D. N.",
            }
          }
        />
      </li>
      <li>
        <a
          className="link"
          href="/word/genja"
          onClick={[Function]}
          onMouseEnter={[Function]}
          onTouchStart={[Function]}
        >
          genja
        </a>
        <p
          dangerouslySetInnerHTML={
            {
              "__html": "u, f. [gana], an απ. λεγ.: poët. <i>an axe,</i> Edda (Gl.): the name of a ship, Branda-genja, Sturl. ii. 166: the nickname of a woman, id.",
            }
          }
        />
      </li>
      <li>
        <a
          className="link"
          href="/word/genta"
          onClick={[Function]}
          onMouseEnter={[Function]}
          onTouchStart={[Function]}
        >
          genta
        </a>
        <p
          dangerouslySetInnerHTML={
            {
              "__html": "u, f. <i>a girl;</i> a special Norse word not used in Icel. and not found in old writers earlier than the 14th or 15th century, unless ‘getta’ in the verse in Hálfs S. be the same or kindred word; hvað líðr nú grautnum, genta, Fas. iii. 382, 389, 393 (Eg. Einh. S.), is the only passage where it is used in Icel. writers. In many languages the equivalents are curious, Engl. <i>girl,</i> Swed. <i>flicka,</i> Norse <i>genta</i> (proncd. <i>yenta,</i> vide Asbjörnsen’s Tales), Icel. <i>stúlka,</i> Dan. <i>pige,</i>—all words that baffle etymologers. The Germans seem to have no such word, as <i>mädchen</i> and <i>mädel</i> are plain enough. The Lapps have <i>ganda</i> = <i>a girl,</i> which may be the origin of genta.",
            }
          }
        />
      </li>
