        >
          heim-dalr
        </a>
        <p
          dangerouslySetInnerHTML={
            {
              "__html": "m., with single <i>l</i>, not Heimdallr, as shewn from the gen. -dalar, not -dalls; a later form used in the Rímur was Heimdæl-l, Þrymlur 1. 8:—<i>the god Heimdal,</i> Edda, whence the poem <strong>Heimdalar-galdr,</strong> m. id. The etymology has not been made out: Heimdal was the heavenly <i>watchman</i> in the old mythology, answering to St. Peter in the medieval legends; respecting him vide Edda 17 (Sksm.) and passim, Gm. 13: he was also regarded as the father and founder of the different classes of mankind, see Rm. and Vsp. 1,—meiri ok minni mögu Heimdalar, <i>the higher and lower sons of H.,</i> i. e. <i>all men.</i>",
            }
          }
        />
      </li>
