      </li>
      <li>
        <a
          className="link"
          href="/word/herad"
          onClick={[Function]}
          onMouseEnter={[Function]}
          onTouchStart={[Function]}
        >
          hérað
        </a>
        <p
          dangerouslySetInnerHTML={
            {
              "__html": "or <strong>hierat,</strong> n., pl. héruð or héröð, spelt hieroþ in the vellum, 656 C. 9, 673 A. 53, and in O. H. L. Cod. Upsal. hærað, see p. 113: [hérað is undoubtedly derived from herr (A. S. <i>here</i>), <i>a host</i>, and not from hér, <i>here;</i> the long vowel (<i>é</i>) is prob. caused by the characteristic <i>j</i> in her-r (herj-); so that hierað (hérað), through the after effect of the <i>i</i> sound, stands for heriað; cp. Dan. <i>herred,</i> Swed. <i>härad:</i> the Old Engl. and Scot. law term <i>heriot</i> may also be connected with the Scandin. word, in which case the original sense of hérað might be a <i>tax</i> to be paid to the lord in lieu of military service: the inflex. -að is derived from auðr, óðal, as has been suggested by the old commentators, e. g. Björn á Skarðsá]:—<i>a county, district:</i>",
            }
          }
        />
      </li>
