      <li>
        <a
          className="link"
          href="/word/jol"
          onClick={[Function]}
          onMouseEnter={[Function]}
          onTouchStart={[Function]}
        >
          jól
        </a>
        <p
          dangerouslySetInnerHTML={
            {
              "__html": "n. pl., in rhymes, <i>gól</i>ig, <i>Jól</i>a, Ó. H. (in a verse); [A. S. <i>geôl,</i> sometimes used of the whole month of December, whereas December is also called <i>æra geola</i> = <i>fore Yule,</i> and January <i>æftera geola</i> = <i>after Yule;</i> the plur. in Icel. perhaps refers to this double month. The origin and etymology of the word Yule is much contested, and has been treated at length by Grimm (Gesch. der Deutschen Sprache), who tries to make out a relation between the Lat. <i>Jūlus</i> or <i>Jūlius</i> and the Teut. <i>Yule,</i> the one being a midsummer month, the other a midwinter month; like former etymologists, he also derives the word from hjól, <i>a wheel,</i> as referring to the sun’s wheeling round at midwinter and midsummer time. The resemblance of the words is striking, as also the old northern celebration of the midsummer feast Jónsvaka (see below), which was in fact a kind of midsummer Yule.]",
            }
          }
        />
      </li>
