              "__html": "u, f., gen. pl. kistna, Nj. 20; [A. S. <i>kist;</i> North. E. and Scot. <i>kist;</i> Engl. <i>chest;</i> Dan. <i>kiste;</i> from Lat. <i>cista</i>]:—<i>a chest,</i> Eg. 310; í kerum eða kistum, N. G. L. i. 383; kómu til kistu kröfðu lukla, Vkv.; kistur ok hirzlur, 656 B. 1, passim; kistu lok, botn, lykill, <i>the cover, bottom, key of a chest,</i> Nj. 94; kistu-fjöl, <i>a chest board,</i> Hom. 155; kistu-hringr, <i>a ring in a chest,</i> Fms. x. 258; kistu-þrum, <i>the old shell of a chest,</i> Pm. 64, 73: <i>a coffin</i> (usually lík-kista), Eg. 126, 127, Grág. i. 207, Bs. i. 337, Fs. 132; kemba ok þerra áðr í kistu fari, Sdm. 34; knörr mun ek kaupa ok kistu steinda, Ám. 101, see Worsaae, No. 504: <i>the seat</i> in the poop of a ship (cp. hásætis-k.), Orkn. 400, Fms. vii. 201: the word, although foreign, is old, as it occurs in old poems such as Vkv., Sdm., Am. <strong>kistna-smiðr,</strong> n. <i>a joiner,</i> Rétt. 2. 10, N. G. L. ii. 246. <strong>kistu-leggja,</strong> lagði, <i>to lay in a coffin.</i>",
            }
          }
        />
      </li>
