      <li>
        <a
          className="link"
          href="/word/sonr"
          onClick={[Function]}
          onMouseEnter={[Function]}
          onTouchStart={[Function]}
        >
          sonr
        </a>
        <p
          dangerouslySetInnerHTML={
            {
              "__html": "m., gen. sonar, dat. syni, pl. synir, acc. sonu, and mod. syni, which form occurs in vellums of the end of the 13th century (Cod. Fris.), Sks. 329 B: an acc. sing. sunu, Thorsen 335 (a Dan. Runic stone). The forms syni, synir refer to an older nom. <strong>sunr,</strong> which is freq. in Norse vellums; on the other hand, Icel, vellums now and then have dat. søni, pl. sønir, Grág. ii. 174; senir, 656 C. 14; Guðs senir, id.; even spelt seyni, seynir, Bs. i, Rafns. S.; the ø (<i>ey</i>) representing the vowel change of <i>o</i>. When sonr is suffixed to a name, the Icel. (but not Norsemen) drop the <i>r</i>, e. g. Snorri Sturlu-son (not sonr); it is in Edd. written in one word, Árni Magnússon, but in the vellums in two words, as in the list D. I. i. 185, 186 (Fiðr Halls son, Hjalti Arnsteins son …): [the root is su<i>nu</i>; cp. Goth. <i>sunus,</i> A. S. <i>sunu,</i> whence Engl. <i>son</i>, Dan. <i>sön,</i> but <i>sen</i> when suffixed, as <i>Peter-sen.</i>]",
            }
          }
        />
      </li>
